    "proof_tex": "For orthonormal matrices define\n\\[\n d_0(Q_1,Q_2)=\\min_{R\\in\\mathbb O_r}\\lVert Q_1R-Q_2\\rVert_{2,\\infty}.\n\\]\nRight multiplication by an orthogonal matrix is an isometry for every row norm.  If \\(R_{12}\\) and \\(R_{23}\\) are minimizing sequences, their product is admissible for the first-to-third comparison, so the ordinary triangle inequality gives the quotient triangle inequality for \\(d_0\\).  Multiplication by \\(\\sqrt{n/(\\mu r)}\\) shows that \\(\\mathcal L\\) has the same triangle and reverse-triangle inequalities.\n\nThe loose blind expansion gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm B},U^{\\mathrm B})=O_P(d_{nm}).\n\\]\nThe loose recorded-IPW theorem has a no-larger order because \\(q<1/2\\) and the definition of \\(d_{nm}\\) dominates its two terms.  Hence\n\\[\n \\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I},U)=O_P(d_{nm}).\n\\]\nApplying the reverse triangle inequality first to \\((\\widehat U^{\\mathrm B},U^{\\mathrm B},U)\\) and then to \\((\\widehat U^{\\mathrm B},\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I},U^{\\mathrm B},U)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\left|\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm B},U)-\\rho_{nm}\\right|\n &\\le\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm B},U^{\\mathrm B}),\\\\\n |\\widehat\\rho_{nm}-\\rho_{nm}|\n &\\le\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm B},U^{\\mathrm B})\n +\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I},U).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis proves both \\(O_P(d_{nm})\\) assertions.  If \\(d_{nm}=O(b_{nm})\\), each displayed variable differs from \\(\\rho_{nm}\\) by \\(O_P(b_{nm})\\).  The three equivalences follow in both directions by the triangle inequality.  Dividing the second display by \\(b_{nm}\\) also shows that divergence of the observable discrepancy forces divergence of the blind loss whenever the resolution condition holds.",
